"Emphasizes stations, route continuity and transfer opportunities while deliberately simplifying geographic shape and distance.\n\nUse when: Help passengers choose a route and identify transfers. Explain the structure of a public transport network at a glance.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need exact walking distance, travel time or geographic navigation from the schematic alone.\n\nVisual grammar: Lines represent named services or routes. Station marks show served stops; distinct symbols identify genuine interchanges. Schematic geometry preserves connectivity while a legend explains geographic simplification.\n\nBuild: List routes, stop order and actual interchange rules. Arrange lines with consistent direction changes and short station labels. Separate dense service families or areas while preserving all real connections.\n\nCheck: Station order matches the real service. Crossing lines are not marked as transfers unless interchange exists. Use separate views for dense crossings without inventing or removing network links.\n\nPitfalls: Connecting nearby stations that have no permitted interchange. Implying equal diagram spacing means equal distance or journey time.\n\nExample: A city rail schematic highlights two transfer stations, keeps nonconnecting crossings distinct and provides a separate geographic view for walking context.\n\nAlternatives: Reference map, Geographic flow map."
